#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:1
msgid "Being trusted with the numbers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:3
msgid ""
"Extracting data from documents designed for human eyes is not exact. Any "
"tool that says otherwise is misrepresenting itself, and a tool whose "
"output feeds decisions about money owes its users something better than "
"either a disclaimer or a boast."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:7
msgid "The two easy answers, and why neither is taken"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:9
msgid ""
"**Take no responsibility.** Ship a licence that makes checking the output"
" entirely the user's problem. Cheap, fast, and it moves the whole burden "
"onto the person least able to carry it — someone who would have to re-"
"read the PDFs to check, which is what they came here to avoid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:13
msgid ""
"**Take total responsibility.** Publish only what has been verified to a "
"standard nobody can dispute. Also unworkable: it slows the work to the "
"speed of manual review and ends up reproducing data that commercial "
"databases already sell, months later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:17
msgid ""
"This project sits near the second end without pretending to reach it. The"
" commitment is not \"the output is correct\" but: **the method is "
"published, the claims are attributable, and the limits are stated**. A "
"user who wants to rely on a number can find out how it was produced, who "
"vouched for it, and under which protocol — and can then decide, which is "
"a decision they can only make if the information exists."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:23
msgid "How a claim is recorded"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:25
msgid ""
"The mechanism is deliberately small. It is a **grant**: a record that a "
"named contributor vouches for specific files, under a specific published "
"methodology, at a specific content hash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:28
msgid ""
"A contributor has one **validation document**, a YAML file in the "
"repository's `validation/` directory, holding: who they are and their "
"public key; which methodologies they are using, each pinned by the hash "
"of the page describing it; the files granted under each, each pinned by "
"the hash of its own content; and a cryptographic signature over the whole"
" thing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:33
msgid ""
"Everything in that structure is content-addressed, and that is the whole "
"idea. If a granted file changes, its hash no longer matches and the grant"
" visibly no longer applies to what is on disk. If a *methodology page* "
"changes, every grant that cited it is invalidated, because the claim was "
"made about that text and the text is now different. The document itself "
"names the version of the general methodology it was written under, by the"
" hash of that page — so the meaning of the entries cannot drift out from "
"under them either."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:40
msgid ""
"This has a practical consequence worth stating plainly, since it catches "
"people: **the validation pages in this documentation are not editable "
"prose.** Correcting a typo in one of them invalidates signed grants in "
"repositories maintained by other people. Changing them is a deliberate "
"operation — re-granting and re-signing — not an act of tidying."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:45
msgid "Where the rest of this lives"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:46
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"This page is the position, in brief. The argument behind it — why a "
"methodology is a document at an address you chose to trust, and what each"
" published methodology claims — is {doc}`../guides/institutional/why-"
"trust-a-grant`. Making a grant yourself is {doc}`../guides/grants/index`."
" The methodologies themselves, which are hashed artefacts rather than "
"ordinary prose, are {doc}`../validation/index`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:52
msgid "What this does not give you"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/overview/trust.md:54
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"It does not make the extraction correct. It makes the extraction "
"**accountable**, which is a different and smaller thing: you can find out"
" what was claimed, by whom, on what basis, and about exactly which bytes."
" Where that is not enough for your purpose, the honest answer is that it "
"is not enough, and the {doc}`validation section <../validation/index>` is"
" written to let you determine that for yourself rather than to reassure "
"you."
msgstr ""

